the S&amp;P or the Dow here,

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:40.590
that's up big today, almost
about 3% earlier today.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.420
Now, how did we get here?

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.730
We put out a report last week,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.830
or a couple weeks ago it
might have been at this point,

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.960
on how Silicon Valley Bank's collapse

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.360
is impacting the overall
global banking sector.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.380
Make sure you check that out.

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:53.220
We're gonna have that linked

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:56.760
so you can easily find that
when you're watching on VOD.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:03:57.840
But check that out.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.150
Basically, the big problem
was Silicon Valley Bank

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.740
made some poor decisions

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.320
in not hedging a lot of
its bets on treasuries,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.690
but the big problem for
the overall banking sector

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.990
is Silicon Valley Bank wasn't
doing anything different

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:11.580
than what other banks are doing.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:12.510
A lot of the assets,

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:15.120
basically any asset that you
purchased about a year ago

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.020
is gonna be worth less than
what it was at that point now.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.160
So a lot of these banks,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.547
when people are returning and saying,

00:04:21.547 --> 00:04:23.670
"Hey, I want my money
back, I need my deposits,"

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:24.870
they're saying, "We don't have it.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.460
We've put this in these assets

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.020
and these assets are worth less

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.340
than what we paid for them now,

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.560
so if we were to sell them,
we'd have to sell at a loss,

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.150
and then we would have less money

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.190
than what we got in deposits."

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:36.240
That's a big problem,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.920
and if there were runs on all these banks,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.820
the big five, US Bank,
Wells Fargo, JP Morgan,

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.760
Bank of America those guys
generally would be all right.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.260
And Citigroup, you throw them in there.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.260
But for other banks, there
really isn't a bright light.

00:04:51.442 --> 00:04:52.620
If they get bank runs,

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.420
they're not gonna be able to fulfill those

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.680
because the things they've invested in,

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:56.550
whether that's mortgages,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.920
whether it's mortgage-backed
securities, US treasuries,

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.220
which are very safe, have all
lost value over the past year.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:03.690
And treasuries especially,

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:06.390
the price of treasuries
has sunk significantly.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:08.670
So that gets us to the role of the Fed,

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.830
because the Federal Reserve
has really stepped in here

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.533
to play a big role making
sure all this happens.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.680
We talk about the Fed a lot,

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.710
and the Fed has injected a lot
of liquidity into the system.

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.600
Fed Vice Chair for
Supervision Michael Barr

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.320
will be testifying, as well as
FDIC chair Martin Gruenberg,

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.900
and Treasurer Under Secretary
for Domestic Finance

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.780
Nellie Liang will testify
at a pair of hearings,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.670
Senate Banking Committee on Tuesday,

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.460
and the House Financial
Services Committee on Wednesday.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:36.990
So those are gonna be big.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:38.220
You're also gonna watch out for

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.440
in terms of the overall
picture for central banks,

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.210
European Central Bank President Lagarde,

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:43.710
she's gonna speak on Tuesday,

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.415
and then on Friday, you get
eurozone inflation figures.

00:05:47.415 --> 00:05:49.784
Now, that number was 8.5%,

00:05:49.784 --> 00:05:52.320
you saw 8.5% year-over-year inflation

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.690
in the eurozone for the last month.

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:55.920
That was back in February.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.890
For March, if that number is up above 8%,

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.260
that's gonna signal that
inflation is not falling.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:02.820
Remember, the ECB raised rates

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.830
50 basis points at their last meeting.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.180
That's a very big hike for them.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.240
Remember, rates were negative
just about a year ago,

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.804
and this increase now is they're
moving at a very fast pace

00:06:12.804 --> 00:06:14.250
and saying, "We're not worried about

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.600
this banking sector thing.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:16.590
We're gonna keep raising rates

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.090
because we have to fight inflation."

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.250
If inflation comes in hot this week

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.290
on that number for eurozone inflation,

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.320
you could see more worries about
the overall banking system,

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.600
the health of the economy,
all that good stuff.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.180
On the other side of the
coin, here in the US,

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:33.330
the Federal Reserve has taken
some pretty significant steps

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:34.800
to try to shore up the banking sector.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.750
And I wanna throw something
up here on your screen

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.130
to just kind of illustrate

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.740
what we have seen from
the Federal Reserve,

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.480
and the overall state of things

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.250
and how the Fed has operated.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.730
Now, you're seeing that, what
you see is starting at 2020,

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:49.200
the Fed just buying up everything,

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.610
what we called QE infinity.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:52.170
And that number kinda goes up and up,

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:53.790
and then it starts to come down

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:55.080
as the Fed starts raising rates,

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.720
implementing quantitative
tightening or QT.

00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:59.520
Basically, not buying any more bonds

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:02.010
and actually letting the
ones that they own mature

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.090
and not buying them back.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.520
But then at the edge of the
right side of your screen,

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.560
you see that line that
goes basically straight up?

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.540
That's what's happened
over the last couple weeks

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.120
because the Fed has had
to try and pour some water

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:14.460
on this financial crisis
or this banking crisis,

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.800
and that's added them add a lot more

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.660
to their balance sheets.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.340
And we're seeing that number pick up

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.770
back towards the $9 trillion figure,

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.290
which is where the Fed
had purchased bonds,

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.870
not even, let's say five, six months ago,

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.730
before they started
quantitative tightening.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:31.080
So wanted to put that up there

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.870
just to illustrate the
overall rate of things

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.720
and how this banking crisis has adapted.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.520
We'll hear from Fed Vice Chair, or Fed,

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.570
excuse me, New York Fed President
John Williams on Friday,

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:44.337
and he's gonna be kinda the
most influential speaker

00:07:44.337 --> 00:07:47.730
that we'll hear from after
the Fed's meeting last week.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:49.590
Listen, let's talk inflation
and recession worries,

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:51.810
because those are obviously
on people's minds.

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:52.740
We get The Conference Board

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:55.650
and the University of Michigan's
Consumer Confidence Index

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.450
and the Consumer Sentiment Index.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:07:59.100
Those are both coming out this week.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.780
A little bit about the
difference between the two,

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.670
The Conference Board is more about

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:03.960
how people feel about business.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.370
It's more about how folks

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:06.817
are thinking about the state of business,

00:08:06.817 --> 00:08:09.810
how easy it's to do business,
how difficult what they think,

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:11.340
how they think businesses are doing,

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.227
whereas the University of
Michigan survey basically asks,

00:08:14.227 --> 00:08:15.390
"Hey, how are you feeling?

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:16.980
How's your financial house?

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.170
How is everything, it going for you?"

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.760
And when we look at those,

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.370
really the most important
one I think for consumers,

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:24.840
and for what folks are gonna be doing,

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:26.460
where they're gonna
continue to spend money,

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:27.930
whether they're gonna keep buying things

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:29.400
is the University of Michigan.

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:30.840
That has been trending up this year,

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:33.600
but we've seen some worrisome indicators.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.670
And also, how much has this banking crisis

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:36.510
weighs down the consumers.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:39.720
So wanna put a chart up here
on your screen really quickly.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.360
It's the University of Michigan's
Consumer Sentiment Index.

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:45.360
You see it kinda went
down going into 2023,

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.060
but over the last couple
months, we've seen that pick up.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.160
And will we see that continue?

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:51.483
That's the big question.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:55.980
We are seeing a big decrease
from The Conference Board

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:00.840
saying the second consecutive
month their index decreased.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:02.520
This is different from the
University of Michigan Index

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:03.450
which is up on your screen.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:04.440
Wanna take that down

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:05.940
because I think we've
illustrated our point there

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:06.990
